what = IntroWhat(
  "Most manual presentations fail the same way: someone reads release notes aloud over an architecture diagram while the real audience — the people who must click buttons at 2am — silently disengages. This realm teaches the opposite: analyze the audience first, walk one realistic scenario end-to-end in the real UI, keep a fallback for the live demo, and hand everyone a permanent permalink to a docs site.",
  "Presenting a user manual is the craft of turning written documentation for an IT system, Web App, or Developer Experience (DX) into a live, audience-shaped story — a guided walkthrough of one real task, proven in the running product, and backed by durable, findable reference material.",
  [
    "Audience First: end users, operators/admins, and developers each need a completely different cut of the same manual",
    "One-Scenario Arc: hook with the user's pain, walk one persona task end-to-end, show gotchas, end with a permalink",
    "Markdown-Native Tooling: Marp, Slidev, and reveal.js keep slides in the same Git repo as the code",
    "Docs-as-Code Delivery: Docusaurus, MkDocs, and VitePress turn the manual into a searchable, versioned web app",
    "Live Demo with a Safety Net: every live demo of the product ships with a pre-recorded OBS fallback clip",
  ],
  [
    "Technical Communication",
    "Documentation",
    "Presentation",
    "Developer Experience",
    "Docs-as-Code",
  ],
)

why = IntroWhy(
  "A manual nobody watches or finds is a manual that doesn't exist. Teams spend weeks writing documentation, then present it in the format least likely to stick — walls of text read aloud — and then wonder why the support queue never shrinks.",
  "Presentations grounded in one realistic task and delivered with the right instrument (a Marp deck for a release webinar, a Swagger UI playground for API consumers, an in-app tour for self-serve onboarding) measurably cut support tickets, speed up onboarding, and make the manual self-service instead of person-service.",
)
